"""Original foundations drill; the first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
Physical education is best described as|an educational process using movement and physical activities|training only elite athletes for tournaments|teaching health facts without movement|recording sports results without teaching|Physical education uses movement to support learning, skills and attitudes.
Health education can help people|develop knowledge and skills for informed health decisions|guarantee that all diseases are prevented|replace all qualified medical care|make environmental factors irrelevant to health|Health education supports informed decisions but does not guarantee outcomes.
Physical activity involves bodily movement produced by|skeletal muscles using energy|cardiac muscle alone without bodily movement|all movements of internal organs regardless of energy use|gravity acting on an inactive body without muscular effort|Physical activity involves skeletal-muscle movement and energy expenditure.
Exercise is commonly described as physical activity that is|planned and structured to maintain or improve fitness|always unplanned and incidental|necessarily organised as competition|never repeated across sessions|Exercise is planned and structured for a fitness purpose.
A scheduled walking session to improve fitness is|exercise and physical activity|physical activity but not exercise because walking is not vigorous|sport by definition but not physical activity|neither exercise nor physical activity|Planned walking for fitness is exercise and also physical activity.
Walking to school can be|physical activity even if it is not a planned exercise session|exercise in every case regardless of purpose|necessarily organised competitive sport|not physical activity unless supervised|Walking uses skeletal muscles and energy even when it is not planned exercise.
Sport commonly involves|activities governed by rules and often organised competition|only unstructured daily transport|only non-physical study of health|movement that can never involve rules|Sport generally involves activities under rules, often organised practice or competition.
Which statement about PE and sport is most accurate?|PE can use sport but is not limited to elite competition|PE is restricted to selecting elite athletes|sport and PE have no possible overlap|a game score measures all PE learning|PE can include sport while pursuing broader learning outcomes.
Explaining why a warm-up is used is most directly a|cognitive learning outcome|primarily a motor performance outcome|primarily a social cooperation outcome|primarily a measurement of strength|Explaining a principle demonstrates understanding.
Improving a movement skill is most directly a|physical or motor outcome|primarily a cognitive explanation outcome|primarily a social negotiation outcome|primarily an emotional confidence outcome|Movement skills are physical or motor learning outcomes.
Learning to cooperate with teammates is most directly a|social outcome|primarily a motor performance outcome|primarily an anatomical classification|primarily a physiological measure|Cooperation is a social learning outcome.
Developing confidence in a new activity can be|an emotional learning outcome|a guaranteed outcome for every learner|only an anatomical outcome|only a measure of muscular force|Confidence can be an emotional outcome of learning.
The four areas of learning in PE|can overlap in one activity|must be taught independently with no overlap|exclude movement skills|are relevant only to competitive athletes|Movement, understanding, cooperation and confidence can develop together.
A teacher changes equipment after discussing a learner's needs. This can support|inclusive participation|identical participation without individual consideration|a guarantee that no injury can occur|an automatic clinical diagnosis|Appropriate adjustments can support meaningful participation.
An inclusive programme should consider|the learner, task, environment and safety|a diagnosis without asking the learner|the score independently of the activity|identical equipment regardless of need|Inclusion requires attention to individual goals, context and safety.
A disability label alone|does not determine every person's abilities or preferences|determines identical needs for all people with that label|is sufficient to decide participation without discussion|always prevents all participation|People with the same label can have different needs and preferences.
A safe lesson should include|checking the area and equipment|continuing despite identified hazards|using damaged equipment without inspection|removing appropriate supervision|Checking facilities and equipment helps identify hazards.
A learner reports concerning symptoms during activity. The appropriate general response is to|stop and obtain appropriate assistance|increase the workload to test the learner|continue until the session ends before reporting|assume all symptoms are normal fatigue|Concerning symptoms should be taken seriously and assessed appropriately.
Suitable activity preparation and recovery|can support participation but do not eliminate all risk|eliminate all risk of injury|replace appropriate supervision|are identical for every learner|Preparation and recovery are useful but cannot eliminate every risk.
An educational game may support cooperation as well as movement. This illustrates|overlapping learning outcomes|a physical outcome without any social learning|a social outcome without any movement|that only the winning team can learn|One activity can support physical and social learning together.
A physical education teacher commonly|plans and assesses learning through movement|records only competitive scores|makes all clinical diagnoses independently|excludes all beginners to maintain standards|A PE teacher plans and assesses educational activities.
A coach commonly helps participants|develop skills and performance in an activity|provide clinical care outside their competence|guarantee performance without practice|apply the same training load to all participants|A coach supports skill and performance development within their role.
A health educator commonly supports|learning about health and informed decisions|guaranteeing a particular health outcome|replacing all individual clinical care|restricting health learning to sports scores|Health education supports learning and informed health choices.
Roles of teachers, coaches and health educators|can overlap depending on training and setting|are identical regardless of training or setting|cannot overlap under any circumstances|remove the need for qualified clinical care|Responsibilities vary by training, policy and setting.
A first-aid responder should generally|act within training and arrange further assistance when needed|provide treatment outside their training without seeking help|assume every incident is minor|ignore relevant emergency procedures|First-aid responders act within training and seek additional help when appropriate.
A person's participation can be affected by|cost, transport and accessible facilities|motivation alone in all circumstances|skill alone regardless of facilities|score in the previous match alone|Opportunity and resources can influence participation.
A structured football practice aimed at fitness can be|physical activity, exercise and sport|sport but never exercise|exercise but never physical activity|physical activity but never sport|The categories can overlap in a single session.
A PE programme should assess|learning appropriate to its objectives|only the final score in every lesson|only participation without reference to learning|only performance by the most experienced pupils|Assessment should relate to the intended educational outcomes.
Which is an example of health education?|learning about hygiene and disease prevention|learning a throwing technique without discussing health|competing in a race without a health-learning objective|ranking athletes by their finish time|Health education can address hygiene and disease prevention.
A rule about sharing equipment respectfully can support|social learning|cardiorespiratory endurance alone|joint range of motion alone|the anatomy of the shoulder alone|Respectful sharing can develop social skills.
Learning how to control a ball most directly develops|a motor skill|only memorising a rule without movement|only understanding a health concept|only showing confidence without performing movement|Ball control is a movement skill.
A PE lesson should be adapted to|participants' abilities, experience and circumstances|the strongest participant alone|the same assumptions about every learner|the teacher’s preferred score regardless of ability|Appropriate activities consider the people and context involved.
Which statement about adaptation is sound?|ask the participant and review whether the change works|assume one adjustment works for everyone|remove the activity goal entirely|exclude all learners who request an adjustment|Involve participants and evaluate whether the adaptation supports the goal.
Sport under organised rules and exercise for fitness|can occur in the same activity|cannot occur in the same session|are both unrelated to physical activity|require that every learner competes at elite level|An organised training session may be sport, exercise and physical activity.
A match score alone|does not show every educational outcome of a PE lesson|measures understanding and cooperation perfectly|replaces all other forms of assessment|establishes the cause of every learning difficulty|Learning outcomes extend beyond the result of a game.
A safe activity plan should consider|the environment and appropriate supervision|the final score without considering hazards|team names without examining the setting|spectator numbers without considering supervision|Environment and supervision are relevant to risk management.
Rest and recovery|can be part of an activity plan|must be eliminated from training to ensure progress|guarantee that injuries cannot occur|replace an appropriately planned training load|Recovery is one part of an appropriately planned activity.
Health decisions can be influenced by|resources, environment and support as well as knowledge|knowledge alone regardless of resources|individual choice regardless of environmental conditions|sports performance alone|Choices are affected by access, context and support as well as information.
Which example is primarily a social outcome?|respecting a teammate during a game|naming the femur in a diagram|counting pulse beats after activity|identifying the action at a joint|Respecting teammates is a social behaviour.
Which example is primarily a cognitive outcome?|explaining a safety rule|throwing a ball accurately|balancing on one foot|passing a ball to a moving teammate|Explaining a rule demonstrates understanding.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
